; A declaration macro's TEMPLATE can declare its effects, and before
; 2026-08-25 it was the last declaration in the language that could not.
;
; Three bugs stood between the author and the answer, and each hid the
; next.
;
; 1. `attachAxtags` walked only the TOP-LEVEL declaration vector. A tag
;    written above a `(fn ...)` inside a template has an offset PAST
;    the macro's own span, so it was handed to the next top-level
;    declaration - the INVOCATION.
; 2. `expGiveTags` then gave the invocation's tags to the FIRST
;    generated `fn` and stopped, so the claim landed on a function
;    whose body does not support it.
; 3. And `expBuildDecls` builds a NEW node per template declaration -
;    the node everything after expansion checks - without copying word
;    7, so even an attached tag died at instantiation.
;
; Measured before the fix, on exactly this file: `alpha` drew a false
; AX3010 for a claim it never made, and `beta` - which really does
; perform IO - drew AX3042 with no edit anywhere that could satisfy
; it. A diagnostic no edit can satisfy is worse than no diagnostic.
;
; `loud` is the arm that must FIRE. It is generated by the same macro
; from the same template, performs IO, and carries no tag - so it draws
; AX3042 at its own name. Without it this file would assert only that
; nothing was reported, which a compiler that had stopped checking
; generated functions altogether would also satisfy.
;
; `quiet` is the other control. It is
; generated by the same macro from the same template and performs
; nothing, so it must stay silent: a fix that simply handed the tag to
; every generated function would make `quiet` claim an IO it does not
; perform, and AX3010 would say so.
(import IO)

(macro pair ((pair A B C D)
  (:: A (-> Int Int))
  (fn (A n) n)
  (:: B (-> Int Int))
  ;@axiom:effect(io)
  (fn (B n)
    {
      (println "io")
      n
    }
  )
  (:: C (-> Int Int))
  (fn (C n) (+ n 1))
  (:: D (-> Int Int))
  (fn (D n)
    {
      (println "io")
      n
    }
  )))

(pair alpha beta quiet loud)

(:: main Int)

;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (main) (+ (alpha 1) (+ (beta 2) (+ (quiet 3) (loud 4)))))
